---
import BlogArticle from '../../components/BlogArticle.astro';
import BlogCode from '../../components/BlogCode.astro';

const contents = [
  { id: 'seams', label: 'Pipelines break at the seams' },
  { id: 'start', label: 'Start four services and seed them' },
  { id: 'flow', label: 'Run the whole flow' },
  { id: 'assert', label: 'What the run proves' },
];
const startServices = String.raw`localcloud start --local-only --services gcs,pubsub,bigquery,cloudiam
eval "$(localcloud env)"

curl -fsS -X POST "$LOCALCLOUD_GATEWAY/seed" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/yaml' \
  --data-binary @pipeline-seed.yaml`;
const pipelineSeed = `services:
  gcs:
    buckets:
      - name: raw-events-landing
  pubsub:
    topics:
      - name: gcs-object-events
        subscriptions:
          - name: ingest-worker-sub
  bigquery:
    datasets:
      - name: warehouse
    tables:
      - dataset: warehouse
        name: raw_uploads
        schema:
          fields:
            - name: bucket
              type: STRING
              mode: REQUIRED
            - name: object_name
              type: STRING
              mode: REQUIRED`;
const pipelinePy = String.raw`import json
import os
from google.auth.credentials import AnonymousCredentials
from google.cloud import bigquery, pubsub_v1, storage

project = os.environ["GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT"]
gcs = storage.Client(project=project, credentials=AnonymousCredentials())
subscriber = pubsub_v1.SubscriberClient()
bq = bigquery.Client(
    project=project,
    credentials=AnonymousCredentials(),
    client_options={"api_endpoint": os.environ["BIGQUERY_EMULATOR_HOST"]},
)

# 1. Send object events from the landing bucket to the topic.
bucket = gcs.bucket("raw-events-landing")
bucket.notification(topic_name="gcs-object-events", payload_format="JSON_API_V1").create()

# 2. Upload a file. The bucket publishes the event.
bucket.blob("2026/11/orders.csv").upload_from_string("id,total\n1,42\n")

# 3. The worker pulls the event and writes one warehouse row.
subscription = subscriber.subscription_path(project, "ingest-worker-sub")
response = subscriber.pull(request={"subscription": subscription, "max_messages": 1}, timeout=30)
for received in response.received_messages:
    event = json.loads(received.message.data)
    print(received.message.attributes["eventType"], event["name"])
    errors = bq.insert_rows_json(
        f"{project}.warehouse.raw_uploads",
        [{"bucket": event["bucket"], "object_name": event["name"]}],
    )
    assert not errors, errors
    subscriber.acknowledge(request={"subscription": subscription, "ack_ids": [received.ack_id]})

# 4. Assert on what landed.
rows = bq.query("SELECT bucket, object_name FROM warehouse.raw_uploads").result()
print([tuple(row) for row in rows])`;
const output = `OBJECT_FINALIZE 2026/11/orders.csv
[('raw-events-landing', '2026/11/orders.csv')]`;
---

<BlogArticle
  slug="event-driven-pipelines-gcs-pubsub-bigquery"
  kind="Walkthrough"
  deck="A file lands in a bucket, the bucket tells Pub/Sub, a worker reads the event and writes a row to BigQuery. Run that whole chain on your laptop, in one container, with the same SDK calls you deploy."
  primary={{ href: '/docs/sdk-examples/', label: 'SDK examples' }}
  secondary={{ href: '/services/pubsub/', label: 'Pub/Sub service guide' }}
  {contents}
  outcome="An upload to Cloud Storage that triggers a Pub/Sub event, a worker that consumes it, and a BigQuery query that confirms the row arrived."
  needs="Python with google-cloud-storage, google-cloud-pubsub and google-cloud-bigquery"
  next={{ posts: ['missing-bigquery-emulator-local-sql', 'reproduce-gcp-incidents-locally'], doc: { href: '/services/cloud-storage/', label: 'Cloud Storage service guide: notifications and event types' } }}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="seams">Pipelines break at the seams</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Each service in an ingestion pipeline usually has decent tests of its own. The failures come from the joins: the shape of the bucket notification, an attribute the worker assumed was there, a destination column that doesn't match what the worker sends.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Those joins are hard to test with separate tools, because the thing under test is the handoff. LocalCloud runs <a href="/services/cloud-storage/">Cloud Storage</a>, <a href="/services/pubsub/">Pub/Sub</a> and <a href="/services/bigquery/">BigQuery</a> in one runtime, and the bucket delivers its object events to the local topic, so the handoffs are real.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="start">Start four services and seed them</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">The pipeline uses three services. The fourth, <code>cloudiam</code>, is there because Cloud Storage checks that it may publish to the topic before it accepts a notification, the same check Google Cloud makes.</p>
    <BlogCode label="pipeline-seed.yaml" code={pipelineSeed} copy="copy-blog-pipeline-seed" />
    <BlogCode label="Start and seed" code={startServices} copy="copy-blog-pipeline-start" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="flow">Run the whole flow</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">One script plays every part so you can read the pipeline top to bottom. In your project the upload, the worker and the assertion live in different places; the SDK calls are the same.</p>
    <BlogCode label="pipeline.py" code={pipelinePy} copy="copy-blog-pipeline-run" />
    <BlogCode label="Output" code={output} />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="assert">What the run proves</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">The first line is the event Cloud Storage published: an <code>OBJECT_FINALIZE</code> for the uploaded object, in the JSON format your production worker parses. The second is the row read back through BigQuery SQL. Between them, the notification config, the message attributes, the acknowledgement and the table schema all had to agree.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">From here, change one side and watch the other. Rename a column in the seed file, or add a field to the row, and the insert reports the mismatch on the spot. Delivery is at least once, as it is in Google Cloud, so a worker that copes with a repeated event here is ready for one there. Keep ordering and volume tests for staging.</p>
  </section>
</BlogArticle>
